# Round 27: custom .desktop template (bundle.linux.deb.desktopTemplate in
# tauri.conf.json) - Tauri's own bundled default (crates/tauri-bundler's
# freedesktop/main.desktop) has neither line this file adds, and there is
# no config-only way to add them; this is the documented workaround
# (confirmed against a currently-open upstream bug, tauri-apps/tauri#16014,
# which recommends exactly this custom-template approach).
#
# IMPORTANT for anyone editing this file: it is itself a Handlebars
# template - do not describe real Handlebars syntax in a comment here
# using literal double-brace mustaches. The bundler's own template engine
# parses this whole file, comments included, before .desktop's "#" syntax
# ever gets a say - a stray, unclosed mustache expression in a comment
# breaks the build with a real, confirmed (not hypothetical) template
# parse error, caught the hard way while building this exact fix.
#
# The Exec line below adds a trailing percent-u field code that the
# bundler's own default template lacks. Per the Desktop Entry
# Specification, a launcher only passes a clicked URL as an argument to
# Exec when that field code is present (singular form for one URL, plural
# for a list) - without it, a real click on a localsync:// link used to
# launch this app with no arguments at all. That's the actual root cause
# of round 27's Linux bug report ("opens to the default Send screen"
# instead of Receive with the code pre-filled) - the app was never
# missing the code, it was never given anything to look for one in. The
# singular field code matches how this app is actually invoked - one
# magic link, one URL - never a batch of them.
#
# The MimeType line below is hardcoded rather than left to the default
# template's own optional variable for it, confirmed not to be populated
# from this project's deep-link scheme config in tauri.conf.json (only
# from an unrelated file-association feature this project doesn't use).
# This is the actual OS-level registration a desktop environment/browser
# reads to know this app is the handler for the scheme at all - see
# postInstallScript below for the other required half (refreshing the
# system's cached copy of it). Keep this in sync by hand with
# tauri.conf.json's own scheme list if that scheme name ever changes -
# there's no single source of truth between this template and a JSON
# config file to enforce it automatically.
[Desktop Entry]
Categories={{categories}}
{{#if comment}}
Comment={{comment}}
{{/if}}
Exec={{exec}} %u
StartupWMClass={{exec}}
Icon={{icon}}
Name={{name}}
Terminal=false
Type=Application
MimeType=x-scheme-handler/localsync;
